(vector (map (:db/id -1)
  (:adr/id "adr-2608301500-four-checks-that-could-not-measure-and-answered-anyway")
  (:adr/title "Checks that could not measure, and answered anyway (cleanup session 2026-08-30)")
  (:adr/status "accepted")
  (:adr/date "2026-08-30")
  (:adr/supersedes "")
  (:adr/related "adr-2608136000-six-questions-before-writing-a-check, adr-2608230300-asking-failed-is-not-an-absent-successor, adr-2607211600-full-history-default")
  (:adr/body
  "## What this session did

A `git cleanup conflict` pass over the superproject and its ~4,316 child checkouts,
then a second pass that fixed a live data defect the first one surfaced. Everything
below is landed on a default branch unless it says otherwise.

The title said `Four` when this was first written. The count reached five inside the
same day, which is the actual finding; the id keeps the original slug because an id is
a discovery alias, not the claim.

### Landed

| where | what |
|---|---|
| root `1e0291eff6ae` | rescued a stranded fleet-ci receipt + fixed the observatory evidence drop |
| root `da9812a13bdb` | COFOG 072 + 033 coverage entries rescued from six dead scout branches |
| root `2b5e7dab6a51` | hourly observatory run carrying the first evidence row |
| root `28878eefb837` | this ADR |
| root `268a5d4b5cbf` | coverage entries stranded outside the closing bracket, + the gate |
| `cloud-itonami/animeka#7` | 1 untracked file |
| `cloud-itonami/eigyo-list#1` | 24 city receipt EDNs + 2 journal entries |
| `cloud-itonami/eigyo-list#2` | the append-only journal delta (disposition recorded on the PR) |
| `net-kotobase/control-plane#578` | 1 untracked file |

Retired: 21 merged remote branches, 10 local, archived first. Preserved by publishing,
never merged: 5 branches that existed only on this machine, one of them
(`kotoba-lang/kotoba`, 117 local-only commits against 2 remote-only) under a new name
because force-push is forbidden.

## The finding

Five separate checks could not make the measurement they claimed, and each returned
the value that means `fine`. This is the class ADR-2608136000 names; the point here is
that it recurred five times in one day across five unrelated tools.

### 1. An observatory mitigation that never once fired

`scripts/observatory-run.cljs` has returned `:signal` / `:load1-before` /
`:load1-after` / `:timeout-ms` from `run-one` since 2026-08-13, in a commit whose own
message is `observatory: record the load, because three timeouts today were my own
parallelism`. `datom` was ready to write all four. The record assembled between the
two never carried them, so `git log -S killed-by-signal` over
`90-docs/observatory/observatory.datoms.edn` returned no commit in the file's history.

It cost precisely what that commit predicted. At `2026-08-30T01:35:08.532Z` six
observatories were written `:known-broken` / `meets-expectation false` after 603-663s
against the registry's 600000ms timeout, at one timestamp, on one machine. One
saturated batch, killed. In the ledger it is indistinguishable from six actors that
regressed. Those rows were not landed. The fix carries the four fields; the next tick
produced the first row in the file's history to have any of them: `meyasu`,
`load1-before 400.96`.

The lesson is narrower than 'add the field'. The field WAS added. Nothing ever
asserted it arrived, so a mitigation sat inert for 17 days looking exactly like one
that works.

### 2. `west-triple-sync plan --names` reads directory names, not remotes

Asked to plan registration for six paths reported as west-unregistered, the plan
proposed three `:register` actions and three `report-skip`s reading `GitHub repo
missing: network-awai/local-murakumo-fastmtp (use new-project-scaffold to create)`.

All six already resolve to repos west carries. The two `etzhayyim` directories are
`:renamed-upstream` (`gh api repos/<old>` redirects to `actor-kizashi` /
`actor-ossekai`, both registered and checked out); the other four are duplicate
checkouts of `cloud-itonami`, `local-murakumo` and `network-isekai`. Applying would
have registered two dead names, added an entry whose `:org` is
`network-awai-worktrees` (a local worktree directory, not a GitHub org), and followed
that `use new-project-scaffold to create` sentence into creating three repos that
exist -- the trap ADR-2608230300 documents by name.

`west-orphan-audit` resolves the git remote and reports `true-orphan-git=0` /
`renamed-UNVERIFIED=0`, exit 0, on the same tree. **Where the two disagree, the audit
is right.** Nothing was registered; `manifest/` was not written.

### 3. The primary-remote fallback picks the annex special remote

72% of `orgs/` checkouts have no `origin`. A fallback of `git remote | head -1` picks
`b2`, the Backblaze annex special remote, because it sorts first. Measured against
`b2`, `gftdcojp/m365-archive`'s `agent/multi-mailbox-ingest` read ABSENT-on-remote
when it was already on `github/`, and a branch archive came out `0 commits, 0B`
because `$REM/main` did not resolve. Prefer `origin`, then a remote whose URL contains
`github.com`, never a bare `head -1`.

### 4. `$?` after a pipe, and a waiter that waited on itself

- `timeout 110 nbb ... > log; echo EXIT=$?` correctly reported `EXIT=124`. Piped to
  `tail` it would have reported 0. Long checks go to a file first.
- `until ! pgrep -f 'cleanup-land.cljs'; do sleep; done` never exits: the waiter's own
  command line contains that string. Four waiters hung on themselves before this was
  noticed. Capture the real PID and use `kill -0`.

### 5. A ledger where the reader saw 23 of 27 entities and reported success

`90-docs/coverage/itonami-coverage.datoms.edn` must be one `[ {…} {…} ]` form. Two
scout commits appended entities AFTER the closing `]` (`a16e9583eae`, whose hunk
`@@ -294,3 @@` ends its context on the bracket, and `edf4bb2efcd`).
`clojure.edn/read-string` returns only the first top-level form, so four entities were
invisible to every consumer while the file continued to read cleanly.

Two of the four (073, 102) were re-adds of codes already inside the vector, identical
but for `:coverage/note` wording -- which is why only `grep` could see a `duplicate`
and the reader could not. The other two (052 waste water management, 053 pollution
abatement) were genuinely new and genuinely unreachable: real measurements no query
would ever have returned.

PR #2689 removed duplicates from this same file on 2026-08-21. It cleaned up without
leaving a check, so the class came back nine days later. The scout prompt meanwhile
promised `the gate will reject re-adding a class that now has a project`, and
`grep -rl itonami-coverage` found no gate reading the file at all -- the promise was
in the prompt, the check was never written.

Gate `root-itonami-coverage` now counts entities two ways -- column-0 `{:db/id` in the
text against what the reader returned -- because one count cannot distinguish `read`
from `read all of it`. Proven five ways against the real defect: main as it stood
(exit 1, 27 vs 23), the repaired tree (exit 0, 25/25), a duplicate inside the vector
(exit 1, DUPLICATE, 26 in text and 26 read, so it took the duplicate branch and not
the truncation one), absent file (exit 2), reader throws (exit 2). Exit 2 is neither
0 nor 1 on purpose. The scout prompt now states both rules.

## Adjacent, and unrelated to the class

**A revert caught before it shipped.** `network-awai/cloud-murakumo` held
`public/go.html` at +33/-224. That checkout is one commit behind, and the commit it
lacks (`c63bfd2`, PR #148) touches that same file: the public operator and contact
disclosure. Not landed, no PR opened, archived with the measurement.

**The west-pin guard denies the whole Bash call.** It is a PreToolUse hook, not a git
hook, so a `fetch && merge --ff-only && push` chain never runs its own sync -- the
command is rejected before its first word and the checkout stays as stale as it was.
`WEST_PIN_VERIFY_SKIP=1` as a command prefix cannot reach it either. The pin it
flagged was never anything the branches did: each branch's `manifest/west.yml` was
byte-identical to its own merge-base. Sync as a separate call, then push.

**This session's archive was deleted by a concurrent session.** A
`/fleet-refactor-wave` run computed its archive path with `rev-parse
--git-common-dir`, which returns a RELATIVE path, so three child-repo archives landed
in the superproject's `.git/stash-archive-20260830/`, and it then `rm -rf`'d the
directory to undo that. Its incident README claims no content was lost; verified
independently, that is true except for one thing. The 21 retired remote branches were
archived with zero-byte diffs against main (their content is on main by definition),
the six coverage-scout branches had every `:coverage/code` confirmed present on main
before deletion, the fleet-ci receipt is on main, and the cloud-murakumo patch was
regenerated from an untouched working tree. **Genuinely lost:** the six
`:known-broken` observatory rows from the 01:35 killed batch, which had been
deliberately not landed and then reverted, leaving the archive as the only copy. They
were evidence-free verdicts about a saturated machine and the observatory re-measures
hourly, so the loss costs a measurement nobody should have trusted. Recorded rather
than glossed. Use `rev-parse --absolute-git-dir`, and never `rm -rf` a
`stash-archive-*` you did not create.

## Resume point

- Nothing is half-finished. Superproject clean and level with `origin/main`, 0
  stashes, 0 worktrees belonging to this session, 0 open PRs opened by it.
  `west-orphan-audit --blocking` exits 0.
- `root-itonami-coverage` has NOT yet run on a fleet node. By this workspace's own
  rule a gate is not landed until it goes green there once; watch for it.
- Watch for: every full observatory tick should now carry `:observatory/load1-before`
  and `:timeout-ms`, and `:killed-by-signal` whenever `spawnSync` reports one. A tick
  that writes `:known-broken` with those nil means the carrier regressed again.
- Not done, deliberately: ~23 pushed-but-un-PR'd branches across 12 child repos, plus
  a 34-branch farm on `network-awai/app-hyakka`. The owner chose preserve-only. The
  runbook gates PR-opening behind `--branches`, which is opt-in.
- Not done: the two `etzhayyim` leftover directories. The correct move is retiring the
  stale checkouts, not registering them.
- `90-docs/observatory/observatory.datoms.edn` goes dirty every hour. That is the
  routine regenerating it, not leftover work; it lands by cleanup pass, not by the
  routine.
- `docs-edn-only verify` reports FAIL on this tree, pre-existing and unrelated:
  `parse-errors=0`, the two `not-tx` entries are other ADRs, the driver is a stray new
  `.md`. That same run declares 233 `.edn` present in git but absent from the sparse
  checkout, so it is partially blind and says so.")))
